<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Deliberately not sandboxed: the companion's on-device tools read the
	     user's files and run their commands, and the sandbox would confine
	     every one of those paths to this app's own container.

	     Full Disk Access is not a way around that, so don't reach for it
	     here. It is a TCC grant, and TCC sits on top of the sandbox: the
	     sandbox grants file access by path list (the container, /System,
	     /Library, the standard folders, whatever the user picked in an open
	     panel) and never consults TCC to widen it, so a grant can only ever
	     remove TCC's veto on a path the sandbox already allows. Confirmed in
	     /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb, which has no TCC
	     rule for file access. Sandboxed, these tools would be limited to
	     user-chosen folders, with the shell confined along with them.

	     The switch that actually decides this is ENABLE_APP_SANDBOX in
	     Runner.xcodeproj (Debug, Profile and Release): Xcode generates the
	     entitlement from it, so turning it back on there re-confines the app.
	     A sandboxed build is what the Mac App Store requires, so this app
	     ships outside it. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
